WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.580
Film Courage: Jeff, regarding screenwriting,
can you talk about the Power of Intention

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:10.860
and how a writer needs to check their own
intention, either during the writing of the

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:14.620
script, before the writing of the script or
afterward?

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:16.160
Jeff Leisawitz: Sure.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:22.390
So we’re all a bunch of humans running around
and (on some level) we’re all pretty much

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:23.800
the same.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:24.800
Right?

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:25.929
Biologically, whatever.

00:00:25.929 --> 00:00:32.870
However, what really separates us is our experiences
and our perspectives because everyone (although

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:33.870
similar) you know.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.890
American culture is similar, let’s say.

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:41.340
We’re growing up in a city or growing up
in the suburbs or these kind of things.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:44.829
What you’ve experienced through your life
and what you’re interested in and what you’re

00:00:44.829 --> 00:00:51.920
educated in, that is what really gives you
your voice, right?

00:00:51.920 --> 00:01:00.449
So if you can tap in to what matters to you
and understand why it matters to you, then

00:01:00.449 --> 00:01:06.930
you can write with power, not only from your
head and imagination, but from your heart.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:12.159
If what you’re writing doesn’t matter
to you, it’s going to be flat.

00:01:12.159 --> 00:01:17.360
It’s never really going to emote.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:22.630
But if you can tap in and understand what
your gifts are and what your perspective is,

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:27.990
what you believe, then you can create characters
and stories based around that, fictionalize

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:32.150
it, embellish it or whatever, (however you
do it), dramatize it.

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:37.040
And then create within a narrative structure
as a screenplay.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.550
And that is where great screenplays come from.

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:44.979
Film Courage: Would someone really write or
spend time on something that doesn’t matter?

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:46.310
Jeff Leisawitz: Yes…[laughs].

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:48.080
Film Courage: It happens?

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:49.080
Okay…

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:53.770
Jeff Leisawitz: I think people do.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:56.630
I think people chase trends…

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:59.850
“Oh, romantic comedies are big this year.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:02.390
Let’s write romantic comedies.”

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:03.650
Okay?

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:06.659
Is that really coming from what you’re passionate
about?

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:09.410
Maybe, but maybe not.

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:18.480
Often I also think screenwriters don’t dig
in deep enough to really understand where

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:19.480
they’re coming from.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:25.200
The pain and the triumph that drives these
stories.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.500
So continually digging deeper.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.360
Asking yourself ‘Why are you doing this?

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:33.190
Why do you care about these characters?’

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:37.830
For instance, one of my screenplays is called
PANACEA’s DREAM.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:45.600
It’s about a shaman and a scientist who
develop this pill that heals anybody.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.740
And they’re making a fortune off of this
thing.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:49.740
And…it works!

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:52.190
But they don’t know why it works.

00:02:52.190 --> 00:03:00.010
The science is driving the scientist crazy
and the shaman takes it on faith (it’s the

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:01.580
power of faith in this pill).

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:09.690
So this is a huge expression for me because
part of me is like “I’m a scientist.

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:10.690
I’m skeptical.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:15.950
I want to know where things are coming from
in the world.”

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:21.650
And the other part believes that this is a
magical world where the metaphysics and the

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:25.230
mysteries are everywhere.

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:27.040
Both of these are within me.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:30.630
And then both of them are in my screenplay.

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:38.620
So by digging into these kind of questions,
these kinds of truths and subjects, a screenwriter

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:43.670
can get a lot deeper into what they believe
and then express is.

00:03:43.670 --> 00:03:47.400
Film Courage: Why do you think a writer has
not dug deeper?

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:48.660
Are they just not that self-aware?

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:52.860
Jeff Leisawitz: I don’t know.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:56.200
I mean, there are all kinds of screenwriters.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:59.570
Some are going to be completely self-aware,
some are going to be completely clueless,

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:04.430
you know (just like people).

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:11.320
But I guess I’m just suggesting that as
you’re considering what to write, these

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:20.440
kinds of questions can really inform and activate
and engage your stories and characters.

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:23.920
If they matter to you, they will matter to
somebody else.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:26.100
And if they don’t, then they just won’t.

